Make echo -E turn off escape interpretation.
echo accepted -E but did nothing with it, so it did not cancel an
earlier -e: "echo -e -E 'a\tb'" and "echo -eE 'a\tb'" wrote a tab,
where bash and coreutils write the backslash and the t. The last of -e
and -E should win.

Clear the escape flag on -E.

---
 coreutils/echo.c |    4 +++-
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/coreutils/echo.c
+++ b/coreutils/echo.c
@@ -117,7 +117,9 @@
 				n = 0;
 			else if (*arg == 'e')
 				e = '\\';
-			else if (*arg != 'E') {
+			else if (*arg == 'E')
+				e = 0;
+			else {
 				/* "-ccc" arg with one of c's invalid, echo it */
 				/* arg consisting from just "-" also handled here */
 				goto just_echo;
